  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Machine safety inspection duties come from PUWER 1998, under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974</li><li>The PUWER guarding hierarchy runs fixed guards, interlocked guards, protection devices, then information and training: use the highest practicable measure</li><li>A guarding inspection covers guards, interlocks, stop and emergency-stop controls, trapping points, safe access, and markings and isolation</li><li>New machinery carries CE or UKCA marking under the Supply of Machinery (Safety) Regulations; the ongoing duty to keep it safe in use is PUWER</li><li>Record the inspection at the machine, with a photo: the checklist does not guard the machine, the guards and safe system of work do</li></ul></div><h2>What a PUWER machine safety inspection is</h2><p>The Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998 (PUWER) require employers to make sure work equipment is suitable for its use, maintained in safe condition, and inspected where needed to keep it safe. It sits under the general duties of the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974. A machine safety inspection is the recurring check that a machine still meets those duties.</p><p>The checklist does not guard the machine. The fixed and interlocked guards, the emergency stops and the safe system of work do. What a good inspection process does is make the check easy to complete honestly at the machine, hard to skip an interlock, and fast to hand back to the office with photo evidence.</p><h2>The PUWER guarding hierarchy</h2><p>Regulation 11 of PUWER sets out how to guard dangerous parts of machinery, as a hierarchy. Use fixed enclosing guards where practicable. Where access is needed, use other guards or protection devices such as interlocked guards. Where guards are not practicable, use protection appliances such as jigs, holders and push-sticks. Where risk still remains, use information, instruction, training and supervision. The point of the hierarchy is that you reach for the highest practicable measure first, not the cheapest or easiest.</p><h2>The machine safety inspection checklist</h2><ul><li><strong>Fixed guards:</strong> in place, secure, and needing a tool to remove</li><li><strong>Interlocked guards:</strong> stop the machine when opened, cannot be easily defeated, reset correctly</li><li><strong>Emergency stops:</strong> latch, cut power, positioned within reach</li><li><strong>Controls:</strong> start and stop controls tested, clearly marked, correctly positioned</li><li><strong>Trapping points:</strong> shearing, crushing and entanglement points guarded</li><li><strong>Safe access:</strong> cleaning, setting and maintenance can be done safely</li><li><strong>Isolation:</strong> isolation points identified for lockout tagout</li><li><strong>Markings and information:</strong> warning signs, operator instructions, guard integrity</li></ul><p>The exact items depend on the machine and its risk assessment. Treat any generic checklist as a starting point, not the whole duty.</p><h2>CE, UKCA and new machinery</h2><p>New machinery placed on the market in Great Britain must meet the Supply of Machinery (Safety) Regulations 2008, carry UKCA marking or CE marking where accepted, and come with a Declaration of Conformity. That is a supply-side duty on the manufacturer or importer, and it is separate from the employer's ongoing PUWER duty to keep the machine safe in use. Marking rules have changed with the UK's regime, so check the current regulations. This is general information, not legal advice.</p><h2>What to look for in a machine safety inspection tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the inspection should be completed at the machine, not typed up later</li><li><strong>Photo evidence:</strong> a defeated interlock or missing guard photographed at the point of observation</li><li><strong>Live dashboard:</strong> a failed guarding check visible to the safety office immediately, not on an emailed report</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes a plant for its office staff. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, with a free tier of 20 reports a month</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, inspector, machine, every observation, for the HSE inspection or insurer that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler records the guarding inspection and the evidence and gives the plant a live dashboard. It does not make the machine safe, and it does not replace a competent PUWER inspection or thorough examination by a qualified person where one is required. It captures what the competent person found, with a timestamp and photo. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your site.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a PUWER machine safety inspection?</h3><p>It is a check that a machine is safe to use under the Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998. It covers guarding, interlocks, emergency stops, controls, trapping points, safe access and isolation, and it confirms the right measure from the PUWER guarding hierarchy is in place and working. The employer must make sure work equipment is suitable, maintained and inspected as needed.</p><h3>What is the PUWER guarding hierarchy?</h3><p>Regulation 11 of PUWER sets a hierarchy for guarding dangerous parts of machinery: fixed enclosing guards first, then other guards or protection devices such as interlocked guards, then protection appliances such as jigs and push-sticks, and finally information, instruction, training and supervision. You use the highest practicable measure, not the easiest one.</p><h3>Does a machine safety inspection have to follow a set checklist?</h3><p>There is no single statutory checklist. PUWER sets the duties, and the specific items depend on the machine and its own risk assessment. A generic checklist is a useful starting point, but the inspection must cover the guarding, isolation and control items that actually apply to that machine. Check the current regulations and your risk assessments. This is not legal advice.</p><h3>Can I run a machine guarding inspection over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's PUWER inspection workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The inspector receives each item in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo of the guard, and the completed report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/factory-audit-software-uk.html\">Factory audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/loto-lockout-tagout-audit-uk.html\">Lockout tagout (LOTO) audit</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/5s-workplace-audit-app-uk.html\">5S workplace audit app</a></li></ul>",
